 1 big thing: Grocery apocalypse 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 America's grocery squeeze has been felt most painfully in expensive categories like beef, coffee and chocolate.

 But new forces are pushing up food prices across the board: expensive grain, soaring fertilizer and fuel prices, plus unpredictable weather and raging wars, Axios' Erica Pandey writes. Why it matters: The next grocery price shock could hit much harder — and wider. And midterms are 65 days away.

 🌎 The big picture: Your grocery store is fed by a vast global supply chain, and pressures are building all along it.

 The Iran and Russia-Ukraine wars have driven up diesel prices , making it more expensive to operate harvesting equipment and to truck food from suppliers to stores. The Iran war has disrupted fertilizer supplies and prices, since a large share of the world's fertilizer passes through the Strait of Hormuz. This past week, corn and wheat prices hit their highest level in three years. Bad weather has hit corn crop yields, and the Russia-Ukraine war has roiled wheat exports. 🌾 Friction point: Wheat's a key ingredient in pantry staples, and corn is a major animal feed. So as they go up, so can meat, dairy and eggs.

 The intrigue: Don't assume those soaring commodity prices are a bonanza for farmers, either.

 They're still getting squeezed by all the other crises. Even the president of the National Corn Growers Association told Axios Future of Energy author Ben Geman that he himself wouldn't be profitable this year. 🥩 Add all these pressures to the list of already inflated food items:

 Instant coffee is up 15.8% year-over-year; tomatoes, 12.8%; beef roasts, 13.5%; apples, 11.1%. In the latest Economist/YouGov poll , about three-quarters of people said grocery prices are still rising where they live. Threat level: There's reason to believe it's only going to get worse.

 J.P. Morgan analysts warned in a report this month that global food inflation would hit 5% in the first half of 2027, nearly doubling this year's rate. "Price pressures are expected to extend through [the first half of 2027], as crop and price effects are still building and agricultural impacts lag the oceanic peak by 6 to 12 months," they wrote. The bottom line: These forces don't drive up your grocery budget overnight. But compounding strains on the cost of growing, harvesting and transporting food could make an already expensive grocery run even more expensive.

