 2. 🏗️ Data centers get blue-collar backing 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Workers build a Microsoft data center in Mount Pleasant, Wis., last year. Photo: Audrey Richardson/Reuters Top tech officials tell me they're optimistic that construction jobs, with powerful building-trades unions as boosters, could help turn the tide on data-center toxicity :

 "Call it the backlash to the backlash," The Wall Street Journal reports . "Unions and construction trade groups are ... warning that [ midterm-driven moratoria, approval slowdowns and new restrictions] endanger thousands of building-related jobs. They are threatening to withhold support [from] candidates opposing the data-center projects." Steamfitters UA Local 602 , whose members — including journeymen, apprentices and helpers — install mechanical piping systems throughout the greater D.C. area, said it's drawing a "clear line" and won't support politicians who oppose data centers, The Journal reports.

 "This is an existential moment for Local 602," the union said in a memo to members. Unionized electricians are attending planning commission meetings to speak up for data-center construction.

 In the Kansas governor's race , a union representing HVAC and railroad workers broke from decades of precedent to endorse Republican state Sen. Ty Masterson over Democratic state Sen. Cindy Holscher, who has called for a moratorium on new data centers. Read the story , "Blue-Collar Jobs Are the New Flashpoint in Data-Center Fight" (gift link). 

 
 
