 6. 👀 CIA chief floats trilateral summit 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Vladimir Putin in Moscow this week. Photo: Mikhail Metzel/AFP via Getty Images CIA Director John Ratcliffe proposed a trilateral meeting between President Trump, Russian President Putin and Ukrainian President Zelensky during his covert trip to Moscow this past week, Axios' Barak Ravid scoops. 

 Why it matters: This was the first time Ratcliffe got personally involved in the diplomatic push to broker a breakthrough between Russia and Ukraine. The Trump administration has raised the idea of a trilateral summit before. Zelensky has been on board, but Putin has rejected it. ⚡ Driving the news: Ratcliffe made a covert trip to Moscow on Tuesday and met with his Russian counterparts — SVR foreign intelligence chief Sergey Naryshkin and FSB director Alexander Bortnikov.

 The New York Times reports that Ratcliffe gave Bortnikov "a bleak assessment" of the state of the war and urged Russia to cut a deal before its situation worsens. What we're watching: Sources told Axios that Ratcliffe concluded that Bortnikov is a constructive player who could help restart diplomatic efforts.

 Ukrainian officials are highly skeptical about Putin's intentions, and say they have intelligence that suggests Putin is planning a major escalation of the war. Keep reading . 

 
 
