 8. ☕ 1 fun thing: Yemeni coffee takeover 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Dubai chocolate cup and Adeni chai from Haraz Coffee House in Niles, Ill. Photo: Monica Eng/Axios Yemeni-American coffee chains have percolated out from suburban Detroit to the Midwest and the rest of the country. Most operate in suburbs with strong Muslim and Arab populations, Axios Chicago's Monica Eng writes. 

 Last year, the number of Yemeni-American coffee shops increased by 50%, making them the fastest-growing coffee sector in the nation, according to Technomic. 🇾🇪 Why Yemen? The Middle Eastern country is considered the birthplace of coffee and still supports small growers, many of whom export coffee to relatives who run U.S. cafes.

